1267. Count Servers that Communicate
Approach: Brute Force + Visited Array
Theory
The problem is to count the number of servers that can communicate with at least one other server.

Two servers can communicate if they are located in the same row or the same column.

Steps:
1. Traverse the entire grid and store the coordinates of all servers (grid[i][j] == 1) in a vector.
2. Create a visited matrix to keep track of servers that have already been counted.
3. For every server:
   - Traverse its entire row and check whether another server exists.
   - Traverse its entire column and check whether another server exists.
4. If another server is found in the same row or column and has not been visited, mark it as visited and increment the answer.
5. Return the total count.
Code (C++)
class Solution {
public:
    int countServers(vector<vector<int>>& grid) {
        int row = grid.size(), col = grid[0].size();

        vector<pair<int,int>> servers;

        for(int i = 0; i < row; i++) {
            for(int j = 0; j < col; j++) {
                if(grid[i][j] == 1) {
                    servers.push_back({i, j});
                }
            }
        }

        vector<vector<int>> vis(row, vector<int>(col, 0));

        int countServer = 0;

        for(const auto& it : servers) {
            int r = it.first, c = it.second;

            for(int i = 0; i < col; i++) {
                if(grid[r][i] == 1 && !vis[r][i] && i != c) {
                    vis[r][i] = 1;
                    countServer++;
                }
            }

            for(int i = 0; i < row; i++) {
                if(grid[i][c] == 1 && !vis[i][c] && i != r) {
                    vis[i][c] = 1;
                    countServer++;
                }
            }
        }

        return countServer;
    }
};


Complexity Analysis
Let m be the number of rows, n be the number of columns, and k be the number of servers.
- Time Complexity: \(O(m \times n + k(m+n))\)
  - Traversing the grid takes \(O(m \times n)\).
  - For every server, we traverse its row and column, taking \(O(m+n)\).
- Space Complexity: \(O(m \times n + k)\)
  - Visited matrix takes \(O(m \times n)\).
  - Server coordinates take \(O(k)\).
Important Note
Your approach works, but it performs repeated row and column traversals.

An optimized approach uses two frequency arrays:
- rowCount[i] stores the number of servers in row i.
- colCount[j] stores the number of servers in column j.
A server communicates if:
\[
rowCount[i] > 1 \quad \text{or} \quad colCount[j] > 1
\]

This reduces the time complexity to \(O(m \times n)\) and auxiliary space to \(O(m+n)\).
